course: biology
lesson: 185
title: Record-breakers: the fastest and the slowest
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Fastest at what? A falcon falling through the sky, a cheetah sprinting, a pronghorn running for many minutes and an ant snapping its jaws are all record-breakers, but each in a different race.
explain: The peregrine falcon is the fastest of all when it dives: it folds its wings and drops at a pigeon like a bullet, faster than a racing car on a straight track.
explain: Speed costs energy and heat, so the cheetah can only sprint for a short dash, while the sloth, whose leafy food gives little energy, saves energy by moving so slowly that hawks hardly notice it.
explain: Some of the fastest movements are tiny: a trap-jaw ant's jaws and a soldier termite's snapping jaws close faster than you could ever blink.
fact: The peregrine falcon is the fastest animal, diving at over three hundred kilometres an hour with its wings folded like a bullet, and bumps in its nostrils help it breathe.
fact: The cheetah is the fastest land animal, running over a hundred kilometres an hour with long legs, a bendy spine and a big heart, but only for a short dash before it gets too hot.
fact: The pronghorn, a deer-like animal of North American grasslands, can run fast for a long time, with big lungs and heart and light bones, and some scientists think extinct American cheetahs once made it so fast.
fact: The sailfish is one of the fastest fish, uses its long thin body and sail to hunt, and changes colour when hunting in groups.
fact: The tiger beetle runs so fast for its size that its eyes cannot keep up, so it stops to look again; for its size it is faster than a cheetah.
fact: Hummingbirds beat their wings up to about eighty times a second to hover at flowers, and they have the fastest heartbeat of any bird.
fact: Mexican free-tailed bats may fly over a hundred and sixty kilometres an hour with long narrow wings, which may make them the fastest in level flight, though not all scientists agree.
fact: Trap-jaw ants lock their jaws open and snap them shut faster than almost any other animal movement, and they can snap against the ground to flip into the air.
fact: Some soldier termites press twisted jaws together until they slip, like snapping fingers, and this snap is one of the fastest movements known in any animal.
fact: The sloth is one of the slowest mammals, because its leafy food gives little energy, and being slow helps it hide from hawks, which spot movement.
fact: Horses are strong runners over long distances, and they can doze standing up because tendons (strong cords joining muscles to bones) lock their legs, ready to run the moment they wake.
fact: A real roadrunner is a bird that runs about thirty kilometres an hour, and a real coyote can run about twice as fast.
exercise: Which is the fastest animal of all? | The peregrine falcon in a dive | The cheetah running flat out on land | The sailfish in the sea | The racehorse on a track
exercise: How does a peregrine falcon reach such speed? | It folds its wings and dives like a bullet | It flaps its wings eighty times every second, like a bee | It rides on storms | It runs along the ground first
exercise: What helps a peregrine falcon breathe at high speed? | Special bumps in its nostrils | A second pair of lungs in its chest | Holding its breath for a minute | Breathing through its open beak
exercise: Which is the fastest land animal? | The cheetah | The pronghorn | The racehorse | The running ostrich
exercise: Why can a cheetah sprint only for a short time? | It gets too hot | Its legs fall asleep | It runs out of spots | It forgets what it was chasing
exercise: What makes the pronghorn special? | It can run fast for a long time | It is the fastest diver in the sky | It is slower than a sloth on a branch | It can only run backwards, very fast
exercise: How does a sailfish's body help it hunt? | Its long thin body and sail | Its heavy, smooth round shell | Its glowing lure that pulls it along | Its long legs for kicking off
exercise: Why does a tiger beetle stop while chasing prey? | It runs so fast its eyes cannot keep up | It gets bored of running after a while and wants a rest | It needs to sleep | Its legs come off
exercise: Why can a hummingbird hover in front of a flower? | It beats its wings very fast, up to about eighty times a second | It hangs from a thin thread of spider silk | It is lighter than air | It stands on the petals
exercise: Which animal may be the fastest in level flight? | The Mexican free-tailed bat | The ostrich | The three-toed sloth | The flamingo of the salt lakes
exercise: How does a trap-jaw ant flip into the air? | It snaps its jaws against the ground | It jumps away with its long back legs | It grows wings | It rides on a bee
exercise: How does a soldier termite make its super-fast snap? | It presses twisted jaws together until they slip | It bites very slowly | It kicks with its legs | It shouts at the enemy so loudly that the enemy runs away
exercise: Why does a sloth move so slowly? | Its leafy food gives little energy | It is always fast asleep | Its legs are broken | It is scared of the ground and of the dark
exercise: How does being slow help a sloth? | Hawks spot movement, so a slow sloth is hard to see | It gives it plenty of time to read | It makes it look much bigger and stronger to hawks and eagles | It scares jaguars
exercise: How can a horse doze standing up? | Tendons lock its legs | It leans against a wall | It never sleeps at all | It hangs from a branch
exercise: What helps a cheetah sprint? | Long legs, a bendy spine and a big heart | Short legs and a heavy, round body that holds lots of water | Wings and feathers | A long trunk
test: In Looney Tunes the Road Runner always outruns the coyote. What is true in real life? | A real coyote runs about twice as fast as a real roadrunner | Roadrunners are faster than cheetahs | Coyotes are slower than sloths | Roadrunners are the fastest birds alive
test: In Zootopia a sloth named Flash works painfully slowly. Why are real sloths slow? | Their leafy food gives little energy, and slowness hides them | They are lazy on purpose | They are always sleepy from drinking coffee, which grows in the jungle | They have no muscles
test: In the film Turbo a snail wins a car race. Which real animal can beat a car on a motorway? | A falcon diving, and maybe a free-tailed bat | A garden snail sliding downhill on a wet, rainy day | A sloth | A tortoise
test: «cheetah's the fastest animal full stop» What is right? | Fastest on land, but a diving peregrine falcon is faster | Right, nothing is faster | No, a sloth is faster | No, a horse is faster
test: «3 cheetahs, 2 sloths, how many legs» | 20 | 12 | 16 | 24
test: «a hummingbird's got 2 wings, there are 4 at the feeder, how many wings beating» | 8 | 4 | 6 | 16
test: What would happen if a cheetah tried to chase a pronghorn for a long time? | The cheetah would overheat and give up, while the pronghorn kept going | The cheetah would win easily after an hour | Both would fall asleep | The pronghorn would turn and chase the cheetah
test: «why's that beetle stopping and starting, is it stupid» | It runs so fast its eyes cannot keep up, so it stops to look | It is lost | It is too tired | It is playing a game
test: «horses sleep lying down only, everyone knows» What is right? | Horses can doze standing up because tendons lock their legs | Right, they always lie down | Horses never sleep | Horses sleep hanging upside down from trees, like bats in a cave
test: Which tiny animal has one of the fastest movements of any animal? | A soldier termite snapping its jaws | A sloth climbing slowly up a tall tree | A tortoise walking | A jellyfish drifting in the sea
test: «hummingbird heart beats once a minute, its so tiny» What is right? | It has the fastest heartbeat of any bird | Right, very slow | It has no heart | It beats only once an hour, so it saves its energy
exam: Identify the animal that attains the greatest speed known in the animal kingdom, and the circumstance in which it does so. | The peregrine falcon, during a dive with wings folded | The cheetah, during a sustained run of one hour | The sailfish, while resting | The racehorse, upon a track
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the cheetah is incapable of maintaining its maximum speed for an extended period. | Its body temperature rises excessively | Its claws become blunt | It lacks lungs of any kind | It is compelled to stop and feed upon the carcass of its prey
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the slowness of the sloth? | It conserves energy on a diet of little energy and renders the animal less conspicuous to hawks | It is the result of injury in all individuals | It is a consequence of permanent sleep | It enables the sloth to pursue prey
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the trap-jaw ant. | Its jaws close faster than almost any other animal movement and may propel it into the air | It is the slowest of all insects | It possesses no jaws | It flies by flapping its jaws
